<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nagabhata I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 730–760</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nagabhata I was the founder of the imperial line of the Gurjara-Pratihara dynasty, ruling in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the mid-8th century from a base probably at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a> or in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalore">Jalor</a> region. Inscriptions credit him with repelling the Arab armies advancing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> around 738, which halted the Umayyad advance into India. His successors, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatsaraja">Vatsaraja</a> onwards, made the kingdom an empire of northern India.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The Gwalior inscription of his descendant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mihira-bhoja">Mihira Bhoja</a> names Nagabhata as the founder of the imperial Pratihara line and traces the family to the hero Lakshmana. The date of his accession is unknown: his grand-nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatsaraja">Vatsaraja</a> ruled in Avanti in 783-784 CE, and allowing 25 years a generation, historians place Nagabhata's accession around 730. Historians such as R. C. Majumdar and Baij Nath Puri hold that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a> was the dynasty's original home. Dasharatha Sharma instead identified Nagabhata with Nāhada, whom a medieval Jain chronicle calls a soldier of fortune who made Jābālipura, identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalore">Jalor</a>, his capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The Gwalior inscription credits Nagabhata with repelling an invasion of mlechchhas, identified with the Umayyad Arab Muslims. The 9th-century historian Al-Baladhuri lists the conquests of the Arab generals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, led by Junayd ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Murri or Al Hakam ibn Awana, but says only that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a> was invaded, apparently a tacit admission of failure. The historian R. V. Somani suggests that the Guhila ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bappa-rawal">Bappa Rawal</a> joined an anti-Arab confederacy formed by Nagabhata.</p><p>Nagabhata appears to have been defeated by the Rashtrakuta ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dantidurga">Dantidurga</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanjan">Sanjan</a> inscription of Amoghavarsha says that during a ceremony Dantidurga held at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>, the Lord of Gurjara served him as pratihara, or door-keeper, a play on the dynasty's name. Several historians, among them D. R. Bhandarkar, identify him with Nagavaloka, the overlord named in the Hansot inscription of 756 CE; if so, he regained his power and ruled the area around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharuch">Bharuch</a> through a Chahamana vassal, perhaps taking it from the Chalukya feudatory <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avanijanashraya-pulakeshin">Avanijanashraya Pulakeshin</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>By the identification with Nagavaloka, Nagabhata ruled until around 760. The Gwalior inscription suggests that he was followed by Kakustha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devaraja">Devaraja</a>, sons of an unnamed brother, and the line passed to his grand-nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatsaraja">Vatsaraja</a>. An inscription of 795 by Gallaka, a subordinate of Vatsaraja, praises Nagabhata I for his victory over the invincible Gurjaras.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nagabhata I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Several Pratihara and Gurjara lineages existed, including the Pratiharas of Mandavyapura near Jodhpur and the Gurjaras of Nandipuri in Gujarat, but the imperial line begins with <b>Nagabhata I</b>, whose base is variously placed at Ujjain or in the Jalor region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
